- op: discuss  # 1205
  at: '2026-08-03T05:52:07Z'  # 1205
  by: agent  # 1205
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1205
  clock: 3  # 1205
  nonce: '1205'  # 1205
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-08-03: EXCLUDE test/fixture/migration-sourced taint flows BY DEFAULT, and DISCLOSE what was excluded in a labelled, counted bucket. Follows the D7 precedent already used for dead-code seeds (a production default plus labelled disclosure buckets) rather than a silent filter. Owner also ruled this goes FIRST, ahead of WI-vazal and WI-kabif section 3a.\n\nWHY THIS ONE FIRST -- it is the only one of the three with a MEASURED VERDICT EFFECT. On the cohort-joruv arm (354 evidence rows, 18 claims, all violated):\n  drop test-sourced  -> 2 of 18 violated claims lose ALL evidence\n  drop ORM (WI-vazal) -> 0 of 18\n  section 3a (pre-registered) -> 0 of 18\n\nAND BOTH FLIPPING CLAIMS ARE CORRECT TO FLIP. Each has exactly ONE evidence row, and I read both rather than trusting the count (L55):\n  pretix/host-secret-no-network       src/tests/e2e/conftest.py       os.environ -> urllib.request.urlopen\n  alertmanager/untrusted-input-no-network  cluster/tls_transport_test.go  bufio.NewReader -> crypto/tls.DialWithDialer\nAn end-to-end test conftest reading an env var and making an HTTP request is not a product security violation. Neither is a TLS transport test dialling TLS.\n\nCORRECTION I OWE THE RECORD, and it is about a claim I delivered to the owner. Last session I reported the two taint-propagator fixes as having \"corrected one claim verdict from confirmed to violated -- a claim reported SATISFIED while being violated\", and used pretix/host-secret-no-network as the headline example of their security value. The verdict change was real; the characterisation was not. That claim's sole evidence is the conftest row above. The propagator fixes were genuine defects and stand on their own, but the example chosen to demonstrate them is test scaffolding. I did not read the flow before reporting it -- which is precisely L55, a lesson I wrote.\n\nSCOPE-AXIS CAVEAT (L1, same shape as WI-vazal's). \"27.7 percent of cohort findings\" is not a cohort-wide property. 74 of the 98 rows are caddy (76 percent), and 64 of those come from a SINGLE FILE, caddytest/integration/reverseproxy_test.go. Remainder: pretix 15, alertmanager 7, kserve 2. Treat the effect size as \"one Go project's integration suite plus a Django project's migrations\", not as a law about repositories.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION NOTES.\n- The predicate already exists but there are TWO of them, deliberately kept distinct by a standing fold-vs-keep ruling (Symbol-level is_test_file versus the broader paths-level notion; the entrypoint predicate is BROADER, not a coarser re-derivation). Pick which one applies and record why; do not assume they are interchangeable.\n- Nobody ever decided taint should ignore test code -- it was simply never wired. That is the tell that this is an unmade decision rather than a considered scope, so the change is a decision being made for the first time, not a regression being fixed.\n- The disclosure bucket is load-bearing, not decoration: a silent drop makes the tool quieter without making it more honest, and the vanished count is exactly what a later session rediscovers as a mystery regression.\n- Re-measure the cohort after landing and re-rank WI-vazal against WI-kabif on the new numbers (owner instruction), rather than on WI-vazal's filed argument, which measurement refuted."  # 1205
