- op: discuss  # 09a0
  at: '2026-08-03T07:26:25Z'  # 09a0
  by: agent  # 09a0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 09a0
  clock: 5  # 09a0
  nonce: 09a0  # 09a0
  message: "DONE. Shipped in PR #182 (dev a37eaf8a80) per the owner ruling. Behavioral evidence below, measured on the production path against a prediction registered BEFORE the run.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED\n- Flows whose SOURCE is test, fixture or migration code are excluded from taint-claim verdicts by default. Classification is on the SOURCE side deliberately: a test reaching a real network primitive is still a test doing its job, and a production function reaching a primitive inside a test helper is still a production flow. The claim asks what the shipped application does, and that is decided by where data enters.\n- Exclusions are DISCLOSED, not dropped. Every verdict carries an excluded_flows bucket keyed by reason (test_sourced / migration_sourced), emitted on CONFIRMED verdicts as well as violated ones. The confirmed path is where a silent filter would mislead most: the claim reads clean and the reader has no way to learn flows existed and were set aside by a policy they did not choose.\n- --include-non-production-sources restores the previous behavior, so this is a default rather than a filter.\n- paths.is_migration_file is new and deliberately NOT folded into is_test_file. A migration is production code that ships and runs once at deploy; it is not test scaffolding. What the two share is only that neither describes the running application. Declared scope: Django **/migrations/, Rails db/migrate/, alembic/versions/, Flyway db/migration/ -- with versions/ claimed ONLY under alembic/ and migrate|migration ONLY under db/, because claiming words that common outright would silently drop real production flows from a security verdict. Anything outside that is an acknowledged gap, not an implied guarantee.\n- Envelope schema 1.1 -> 1.2, additive. Spec updated with the new default and the bump; CLI flag-matrix baseline regenerated.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE (9-repo cohort, cohort-bifob arm vs cohort-joruv, instrument asserted in BOTH directions before measuring and the pre-fix tree could not even run the new signature)\n  claim verdicts     18 violated -> 16 violated, 36 confirmed -> 38\n  FLIPPED, exactly the two predicted by name, no wrong-direction flips:\n      alertmanager/untrusted-input-no-network  violated -> confirmed\n      pretix/host-secret-no-network            violated -> confirmed\n  Each rested on ONE evidence row, and I read both rather than trusting the count:\n      src/tests/e2e/conftest.py        os.environ -> urllib.request.urlopen\n      cluster/tls_transport_test.go    bufio.NewReader -> crypto/tls.DialWithDialer\n\nPREDICTION SCORED HONESTLY -- one half exact, one half stated in the WRONG UNIT. Which claims flip and how many: exact. Volume: I predicted \"98 rows (84 test + 14 migration)\". Actual evidence rows fell 354 -> 270, a drop of 84; the underlying exclusions were 692 FLOW-CLAIM PAIRS (675 test_sourced, 17 migration_sourced). The gap is the evidence cap: pretix/untrusted-input-no-database went 748 -> 140 flows (-81%) yet still displays 100 rows, because removing flows from an over-cap claim changes WHICH rows are shown rather than how many. The 14 predicted migration ROWS never materialised as a row reduction for exactly that reason. I had written this cap caveat into WI-vazal's ruling note an hour earlier and did not apply it to my own forecast. A row-level view understates this change by an order of magnitude on capped claims; quote the unit whenever quoting the number.\n\nSCOPE-AXIS CAVEAT, restated because the percentage invites the wrong reading (L1). Not a cohort-wide property: 74 of the 98 non-production rows are caddy, 64 of those from a SINGLE file (caddytest/integration/reverseproxy_test.go), with pretix 15, alertmanager 7, kserve 2. The effect is \"one Go project's integration suite plus a Django project's migrations\", not a law about repositories.\n\nSIDE FINDING, filed rather than folded. Wiring this needed a symbol-id path-slot parse, and there were already SIX in the tree using three different correct mechanisms, two of them naive and wrong for the one colon-TOLERANT slot in the grammar (ADR-0036 D1a). Added a seventh that is right-anchored and pinned by a test rather than an eighth naive copy; filed WI-ribuz (the class), WI-guror (ir._extract_path_slot, live defect), WI-nukas (profile.py inline), WI-dosir (two docstrings documenting name/kind SWAPPED).\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT DO. It does not touch the ORM question (WI-vazal). pretix/untrusted-input-no-database remains violated on 140 flows after the exclusion, and those are the database-read-to-database-write class -- so ORM is now what is actually holding that claim, which is a STRONGER case for WI-vazal than the argument its own filing made and measurement refuted. Owner instruction stands: re-rank WI-vazal against WI-kabif section 3a on these new numbers."  # 09a0
